# TypeScript practices
- No `any`; use `unknown` + type guards at boundaries. Avoid type assertions unless unavoidable.
- `interface` for object shapes; `type` for unions, intersections, mapped types. Use built-in utility types and generics.
- Discriminated unions for state and results; `readonly` for data that must not change; handle null/undefined explicitly.
- Explicit return types on exported functions. Prefer async/await; never leave a Promise rejection unhandled.
- Naming: PascalCase types, camelCase values/functions, UPPER_CASE constants, boolean names like isX/hasX.
- Keep types next to their use; share them only when they really are shared. Do not add barrel files or a global `types/` dir unless 10-project-conventions allows it.
- Dependency injection for side effects (fetch, storage, clock, DOM), which matches the adapter rule in 10-project-conventions.
